\chapter{Experimental Results}

This chapter presents the experimental results obtained from the implemented active noise cancellation system. The experiments evaluate the spatial response of the microphone array, the computational performance of the real-time processing system, the operation of the programmable filter array, and the performance of adaptive ANC using LMS and FARLS algorithms. The results of the adaptive ANC experiments are then used to investigate the effects of system delay through cross-correlation analysis. Finally, a controlled-delay experiment is used to demonstrate spatial acoustic cancellation using a known signal delay and polarity.

\subsection{Array Processing and Plots}

The purpose of this experiment was to determine whether the microphone-array processing produced the expected spatial response when an acoustic source was moved relative to a specified focal point. For each test, a focal point was selected in front of the microphone array and held fixed while an acoustic source was physically moved toward and away from that location. If the array-processing algorithm correctly focuses on the selected location, the processed signal should have a larger RMS amplitude when the source is near the focal point and a smaller RMS amplitude as the source moves away from it. The variation in RMS amplitude therefore provides an experimental indication of the spatial selectivity of the microphone array.

For each focal-point location, the array-processing algorithm was operated continuously for 15 seconds while the processed array output was recorded. The acoustic source was initially positioned near the selected focal point and was then moved around the focal region during the recording. The source position was not measured or recorded as a function of time. Therefore, the exact source position corresponding to each point in the recorded signal cannot be determined. Instead, the experiment was used to observe whether movement of the acoustic source produced the expected changes in the RMS amplitude of the processed array output.

The experiment was performed using six focal-point locations. The horizontal coordinate of the focal point was varied between $-0.5$, $0$, and $0.5$ m, while the distance from the microphone array was set to either $1.0$ or $1.5$ m. Each focal-point location was tested using both a sinusoidal source and a broadband white-noise source. The two source types were used to determine whether the observed spatial response was present for both narrowband and broadband acoustic signals.

The recorded processed-array signals were analyzed by calculating the RMS amplitude in decibels as a function of time. The RMS amplitude represents the overall signal level of the processed microphone-array output over each analysis interval. Under the expected spatial response, the RMS amplitude should increase when the acoustic source moves toward the selected focal point and decrease when the source moves away from it. Because the source position was not recorded during the experiment, the plots cannot be used to determine the exact spatial coordinates of the source at each point in time. Instead, they show how the processed array output changed as the source was moved around the focal region.

The RMS amplitude response for the sinusoidal source at the six focal-point locations is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:array_sinusoidal_results}. The corresponding response for the broadband white-noise source is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:array_white_noise_results}.

