As shown in the figures, the processed RMS amplitude varies over time as the acoustic source is moved around the focal region. In general, the RMS amplitude increases when the source approaches the selected focal region and decreases as the source moves away. This behavior agrees with the expected response of the array-processing algorithm. When the source is located near the selected focal point, the propagation delays applied to the microphone signals allow the received signals to combine more constructively, producing a larger processed output. When the source is farther from the focal point, the signals combine less constructively, resulting in a lower processed RMS amplitude.

To provide a numerical comparison between the different focal-point locations, the maximum and minimum measured RMS amplitudes were extracted from each recording. The difference between these values was also calculated. This maximum-to-minimum difference provides a measure of how much the processed RMS amplitude changed during the source movement. A larger difference indicates a more pronounced change in the array response, while a smaller difference indicates a less pronounced change.

