For the sinusoidal source, the difference between the maximum and minimum RMS amplitudes ranged from $7.467$ dB to $19.988$ dB. Five of the six focal-point locations produced changes greater than $14$ dB. The smallest change occurred for the focal point at $(-0.5,1.0)$ m, where the RMS amplitude varied by $7.467$ dB. This smaller variation indicates that the spatial response was less pronounced during this particular measurement. The remaining focal-point locations exhibited substantially larger changes in RMS amplitude as the source was moved.

For the broadband white-noise source, the maximum-to-minimum RMS amplitude differences ranged from $13.472$ dB to $16.723$ dB. All six focal-point locations therefore exhibited a change greater than $13$ dB. The relatively consistent variation across the six locations indicates that the array produced a measurable spatial response for broadband signals as well as for the sinusoidal source. This result is particularly relevant to active snore cancellation because snoring contains broadband acoustic energy rather than consisting of a single narrowband tone.

The sinusoidal measurement at the $(-0.5,1.0)$ m focal point exhibited a noticeably smaller RMS amplitude variation than the other sinusoidal measurements. This may have resulted from differences in the source positioning or movement during that particular trial. However, because the source position was not measured throughout the recording, the cause of this difference cannot be determined quantitatively.

The similar response observed for both sinusoidal and broadband sources provides evidence that the spatial selectivity of the array-processing algorithm is not limited to a single-frequency signal. Additionally, measurable RMS amplitude changes were observed at both $1.0$ m and $1.5$ m from the array, indicating that the array-processing method produced a spatially varying response over both tested distances.

It is important to note that this experiment does not provide a complete
two-dimensional characterization of the array response. Because the source position was not recorded simultaneously with the acoustic measurement, an exact relationship between source position and RMS amplitude cannot be established for each point in the recording. Instead, the experiment demonstrates that moving an acoustic source around a selected focal region produces a measurable change in the RMS amplitude of the processed microphone-array output, consistent with the expected spatial selectivity of the array-processing algorithm.

\section{Runtime Performance}

This experiment aims to quantitatively evaluate whether the system is capable of processing data at the rate required for real-time active noise cancellation (ANC). The experiment evaluates the computational performance of the system as processing complexity is progressively increased, from basic data acquisition to the complete array processing and adaptive filtering pipeline.

This evaluation is important because the processing time must remain below the sampling period for the system to operate in real time. If the processing time exceeds the available time between samples, the algorithm will fall behind the incoming data and will be unable to perform ANC correctly.

To conduct this experiment, the Linux-based host machine containing the ADC and DAC cards was used. Because the purpose of this experiment was to evaluate computational performance rather than acoustic performance, the microphone and speaker arrays were not physically connected to the system. However, the software executed the same processing operations as if the full 16-microphone and 16-speaker arrays were connected. The sampling frequency was set to 8~kHz, which was the predetermined sampling frequency for the system.

For each configuration, 80,000 samples were processed while the computational complexity of the algorithm was progressively increased. The tested configurations included sample acquisition alone, processing of a single array, simultaneous microphone and speaker array processing, and finally the addition of the LMS and FARLS adaptive algorithms. The microphone and speaker arrays were configured with 16 channels each. The sinc interpolation filters used for array processing were 61 taps in length, and the adaptive filters contained 400 coefficients. For the LMS algorithm, the adaptation step size was set to $\mu = 0.001$.

