These results indicate that the processing pipeline generally completes within the available time interval for the tested configurations. The increase in processing time as additional processing stages are enabled demonstrates the computational cost associated with each stage while also showing that the complete tested processing pipeline remains below the sampling-period threshold under typical operating conditions.

\section{Programmable Filter Array}
%show plots of the filter working and plots of DAC output
The purpose of this experiment is to demonstrate that the programmable filter array can attenuate high-frequency components of the DAC output while maintaining the modularity afforded by the programmable IC. This capability will allow future work to operate the ANC system with reduced interference from frequencies outside the designated operating spectrum, thereby reducing the burden placed on the adaptive algorithms.

The DAC reconstructs signals using a zero-order hold, which can introduce high-frequency spectral images alongside the desired audio content. The programmable filter array, implemented using the MAX270CPP+ IC and configured as a cascaded fourth-order low-pass filter (as described in Section 3.3), was evaluated to determine its effect on the frequency content of the DAC output. By adjusting the programmed cutoff frequency, the frequency response of the filter can be changed to attenuate frequency components above the desired operating bandwidth.

The experiment was conducted using six programmed cutoff frequencies: 25 kHz, 15 kHz, 10 kHz, 5 kHz, 3 kHz, and 2 kHz. A single tone noise was used for the experiment with the frequency of 500 Hz. The 25 kHz setting represents the highest cutoff frequency available from the programmable filter, while the remaining cutoff frequencies were selected as progressively lower values to evaluate the effect of reducing the filter bandwidth. For each cutoff frequency, the DAC output was recorded and analyzed in both the time and frequency domains. The resulting waveforms and FFTs were used to observe how the programmed cutoff frequency affected the output signal and the attenuation of higher-frequency components.

