The results show that changing the programmed cutoff frequency has a noticeable effect on the frequency content of the DAC output. The measured magnitudes of the 500 Hz input and the largest frequency component above 500 Hz for each cutoff frequency are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:programmable_filter_results}. At the higher cutoff frequencies, such as 25 kHz and 15 kHz, more of the higher-frequency content remains visible in the FFT. For example, the largest component above 500 Hz has a magnitude of $-29.504$ dB at a cutoff frequency of 25 kHz, compared with $-50.432$ dB at a cutoff frequency of 2 kHz. The frequency of the largest component above 500 Hz also shifts from approximately 5.5 kHz at the higher cutoff frequencies to approximately 667 Hz at the 3 kHz and 2 kHz settings. These results demonstrate the increasing attenuation of higher-frequency components as the programmed cutoff frequency is decreased. The time-domain plots also show that the main signal remains present as the cutoff frequency is changed, although the lower cutoff frequencies produce a greater change in the output waveform. Overall, the results show the expected behavior of a low-pass filter as the programmed cutoff frequency is decreased.


The results from the six cutoff frequencies also demonstrate the programmability of the filter array. The cutoff frequency can be changed through the configuration of the MAX270CPP+ without requiring a different hardware filter for each frequency range. This provides flexibility for the ANC system because the filter can be configured for the desired operating bandwidth depending on the signal being processed. For example, a higher cutoff frequency can be used when more of the signal bandwidth is required, while a lower cutoff frequency can be used when greater attenuation of higher frequency components is desired. This allows the same filter hardware to be used for different operating conditions and provides a practical way of controlling the frequency content presented to the ANC system.

\section{Simple ANC with Adaptive Filters}

The purpose of this experiment was to evaluate the ability of the hardware to perform adaptive noise cancellation using a simplified setup. This experiment provides a starting point for evaluating the ANC system before incorporating the full microphone array and spatial processing. By beginning with a simplified configuration, the operation of the adaptive algorithms and the acoustic paths could be evaluated independently of the additional complexity introduced by spatial array processing.

This experiment was conducted using two microphones and two speakers. One microphone acted as the input to the adaptive algorithm, while the second microphone measured the error signal used to update the adaptive filter. One speaker acted as the source speaker and produced the noise signal, while the second speaker produced the cancelling signal generated by the adaptive filter. The input microphone was placed close to the source speaker to minimize the acoustic path between the source and the reference microphone. The error microphone was positioned approximately 0.75 meters from the source speaker, while the cancelling speaker was positioned approximately 0.6 meters from the error microphone.

For this experiment, a sinusoidal waveform was played through the source speaker, resulting in a single-tone input signal. Because the acoustic path was primarily composed of propagation through the anechoic chamber, the resulting adaptive filter was expected to approximate the response required to account for the propagation delay and phase of the acoustic path.

