Overall, the results show that the error signal does not converge despite testing a variety of $\mu$ values and input ranges. In the LMS experiments, even when the experiment was run for 40 seconds, the system eventually became unstable. Other combinations of algorithm parameters were also tested, but the results shown in Figures~\ref{fig:farls_results_sin} and~\ref{fig:lms_results_sin} represent the general behavior observed throughout the experiments. In addition to the error signal failing to converge, the resulting filter coefficients did not correspond to the expected response of the acoustic path through the anechoic chamber. For a single-tone input, the filter response would be expected to represent the required propagation delay and phase relationship between the reference and error signals. The resulting filters did not exhibit the expected behavior.

The FARLS results demonstrate similar difficulties with convergence. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:farls_75_sin}, the larger input range causes the amplitude of the adaptive filter output to increase substantially without reaching a stable solution. Reducing the input range did not result in convergence, although the lower input ranges changed the behavior of the algorithm. Figure~\ref{fig:farls_small_sin} shows an example of the algorithm responding to a change in the error signal at approximately four seconds. The filter coefficients change rapidly in an attempt to reduce the error, but the resulting change does not produce a stable solution. The algorithm then adjusts the coefficients again and temporarily reduces the error. This behavior demonstrates that FARLS is responding to changes in the measured error, but it is not able to consistently identify the required acoustic response and converge to a stable solution.

The LMS results also show a lack of stable convergence. At lower $\mu$ values, the filter coefficients change slowly and the algorithm is unable to reduce the error sufficiently within the duration of the experiment. Increasing $\mu$ causes the algorithm to adapt more quickly, but the larger step size eventually causes the filter to become unstable. This behavior is consistent with the sensitivity of the LMS algorithm to the step size, where an excessively large value can cause successive coefficient updates to overshoot the desired solution and result in divergence.

Although neither algorithm successfully achieved the desired ANC behavior, the FARLS results appear somewhat more promising than the LMS results. FARLS is able to respond rapidly to changes in the error and, in some cases, temporarily reduce the error following a change in the filter coefficients. However, neither algorithm was able to converge to a stable steady-state error or produce a filter response corresponding to the expected acoustic path. Despite testing different algorithm parameters, input ranges, and physical configurations, a stable solution was not obtained. Further testing using a broadband excitation is therefore necessary to determine whether the lack of convergence is related to the single-tone excitation or to another characteristic of the system.


\section{Broadband ANC}

The purpose of this experiment was to extend the previous ANC experiment from a single-tone input to broadband noise. Snoring is a broadband acoustic signal, and therefore the ANC system must be capable of operating with broadband inputs for the system to be applicable to active snore cancellation. For a properly converging adaptive filter, the error signal should generally decrease as the filter adapts to the acoustic environment and determines an appropriate cancellation response. This experiment was therefore used to determine whether the adaptive filters could converge when operating with a broadband input.

To conduct the experiment, a white-noise signal was generated by the computer and output through the primary speaker. A microphone positioned near the primary speaker provided the reference signal to the adaptive filter. The adaptive filter used this reference signal to calculate the cancellation signal, which was output through the secondary speaker. An error microphone was positioned within the acoustic field to measure the resulting signal after the primary and secondary signals interacted. The error signal was then fed back into the adaptive filter to update the filter coefficients. The experiment was operated for 30 seconds, and the resulting error signal was recorded for analysis.

