The results in Figure~\ref{fig:lags_error} show that the measured mean-square error changes as the delay applied to the cancellation signal is varied. As the delay approaches the delay required for the acoustic signals to align at the error microphone, the measured mean-square error decreases. The lowest mean-square value occurs between approximately 26 and 28 seconds in the recording, corresponding to a cancellation-signal delay of 14 samples with negative polarity. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:lags_ms_results}, the 26--28~s window produced a mean-square value of 0.003814, which was the lowest value among the selected time windows. This indicates that the cancellation signal produces the greatest reduction in the measured acoustic response when it is delayed by approximately 14 samples and inverted relative to the source signal.

The delay corresponding to the minimum error can be converted to a time delay using the sampling frequency of 8 kHz. The measured delay is therefore

$$
t_d = \frac{14}{8000} = 0.00175~\text{s} = 1.75~\text{ms}.
$$

This measured delay agrees with the estimated difference in acoustic propagation time between the source and cancelling speakers to the error microphone. The result indicates that the timing of the cancellation signal has a significant effect on the amount of acoustic cancellation produced at the error microphone.

The experiment does not produce complete cancellation because the delay is limited to integer numbers of samples. The actual acoustic propagation delay may fall between two sample periods, resulting in a fractional-sample delay that cannot be represented by the simple delay used in this experiment. Nevertheless, the experiment demonstrates that the hardware is capable of producing spatial acoustic cancellation when the relative timing and polarity of the source and cancellation signals are appropriately selected. A future implementation using fractional-delay filtering could provide finer control of the cancellation-signal timing and potentially produce greater cancellation.

